Test suite for snippet rendering and the dual Plover/nvim artifact pipeline. Tests the full end-to-end stack: template parsing, tabstop renumbering, escaping rules, and the corpus-wide invariants that prevent buffer corruption in the Plover plugin.

The tests load the real `dict.steno` corpus from the repository root, expand it to typed entries, and verify rendering behavior via `render_snippet`, `build_snippets`, and `wrap_inline_body`. Three behaviors are load-bearing: (1) single-landing constructs place their tabstop at `${0}` (the cursor exit), multi-landing ones renumber to `${1}, ${2}, ${0}` in tab order. (2) Non-terminal partials—stubs that will be extended by longer chords—emit plain `{^}...{^}` text with no sentinel, so the nvim plugin never intercepts them; Plover backs up exactly what it typed when the user extends. (3) Family-root strokes (prefixes like `STKWR-PBGS` that are shared by longer entries like `STKWR-PBGS/TPH-FLT`) must never be marked terminal, enforced by the corpus invariant `no_terminal_is_a_prefix_of_another_stroke`. The `` `${%0}` `` template is built directly here because its original stroke was repurposed in the corpus. The `SENTINEL_OPEN`/`SENTINEL_CLOSE` markers delimit the LSP snippet body in Plover's output, allowing the nvim plugin to extract and expand it without a `snippets.json` lookup.